WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.860
Film Courage: So with all the trials and tribulations
with DRIFTER and your goal to make a film

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.730
before age 30, the film eventually ended up
on Netflix?

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.110
Chris von Hoffmann, horror writer/director:
It was a pretty quick turnaround which was

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.519
quite shocking.

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:24.120
The whole first quarter of 2017 of last year
was pretty hectic because I was in the thickness

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.680
of prepping MONSTER PARTY, meeting with all
these actors for that and then DRIFTER was

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.530
screening at UCLA and it finally had its theatrical
run at this little arthouse theater in Hollywood.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:39.820
And then it went to iTunes and Amazon and
all that afterwards and then once we were

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.200
filming MONSTER PARTY, it went to Netflix
in May of last year.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.940
So no one had any more excuses to not watch
the movie.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.860
Then everyone started to watch it.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:55.480
It helped so much that it went to Netflix
and then when it goes to Netflix, you start

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.670
to learn about the darkness of the Internet,
trolls and everyone has an opinion and everyone

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:02.670
loves to comment.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:11.179
At a certain point they all start saying the
same thing and there is no point responding

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:12.179
to anyone of them.

00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:17.270
I just don’t respond to anyone if they’re
obnoxious with comments, it’s like you just

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.929
didn’t get the movie.

00:01:19.929 --> 00:01:23.539
But it definitely helped a lot and then there
were people who totally loved it and totally

00:01:23.539 --> 00:01:25.579
got what the movie was.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:35.869
I got some very nice messages from people
and some not-so-nice messages from people.

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:40.749
But if you’re going to make movies you’ve
got to be thick-skinned, you’ve just got

00:01:40.749 --> 00:01:50.219
to embrace it but it was a very interesting
time.

00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:53.069
I just wasn’t expecting the movie to get
as much exposure as it did.

00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:59.569
I thought it was going to get a small distribution
and just move on.

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:04.999
But then when The Hollywood Reporter is writing
about it and IndieWire is writing about it

00:02:04.999 --> 00:02:11.340
and then it’s getting released all over
the world I’m like “Well…there you are!”

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.719
And every decision you made is all up there
to watch.

00:02:15.719 --> 00:02:20.069
But I don’t regret anything.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:23.040
At the time that was exactly the movie I wanted
to make.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.360
I just wanted to push the soundtrack, push
all that stuff, must maximize the aesthetic

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.090
where you’ve got to pull back for later
movies.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:39.501
With MONSTER PARTY it’s a similar kind of
genre (hybrid storytelling), it’s still

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:42.040
a very different movie at the same time.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:48.610
All the mistakes that I learned on that movie
(that people had no problem telling me about),

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.000
I feel like I learned a lot from.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.860
I always read a handful of bad reviews, you
just want to know what bothers people, what

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:02.310
doesn’t work for people and just fix that
stuff.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.219
Because with MONSTER PARTY we spent a year
on the script.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:13.260
I wanted to make sure that it was an improvement,
if anything else, I just wanted to make sure

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.340
it was an improvement and I feel like it is.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.140
Film Courage: It’s interesting because I
was watching an interview with Margaret Atwood,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.970
it was a BBC documentary or something.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:25.920
And she was talking about her first book (it
blew out the numbers that they had first projected)

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.670
and someone gave her the advice of (and he
just looked at her) “Now they can shoot

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:30.670
at you.”

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:34.030
Especially plus too [her critics asked], “Where
does this darkness come from?

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.989
You must have gone through these things?”

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:38.079
[Her response] “No, this is just what I
write about.

00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:43.079
You don’t have to have experienced them
to go to that level of darkness.”

00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:46.709
So she talked about this as well [harsh criticism].

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.560
Did you think that DRIFTER was going to get
to that level when you were making it or you

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.900
needed to fulfill a goal for yourself?

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:58.319
Chris: I needed to fulfill a goal and I had
no idea it was going to get the kind of exposure

00:03:58.319 --> 00:03:59.599
it got.

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:00.599
It was great in the long run.

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:02.920
I mean it did everything I needed it to do.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:09.239
I had no regrets about any of it.

00:04:09.239 --> 00:04:12.640
If there is just one regret, I wish we spent
more time on the screenplay, that I wasn’t

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.879
so unbelievably…I’m a very antsy kind
of person.

00:04:16.879 --> 00:04:26.139
But now I’ve grown so much as a filmmaker
after that and I changed a lot of how I want

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:27.550
to approach things.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:33.759
And I’ve grown to actually love writing
now and I want to spend a lot of time on the

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:34.759
screenplay.

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:41.750
With DRIFTER I think I was just at the point…I
was concerned with visual construction and

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.300
composition and aesthetic and style because
that movie is all about style.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.069
It’s all about the mood and atmosphere and
the music.

00:04:49.069 --> 00:04:50.800
There is really nothing much more to it.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.970
That’s very much what it’s about.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.830
It’s like a music video or something.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:59.510
And at that time, that was the kind of movie
I wanted to make.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.040
And now I am much more interested in proper
character development, proper narrative.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.669
Because DRIFTER was a very particular kind
of movie.

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:07.610
Even my shorts are nothing like that movie
at all.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:14.069
It was like let me just get this ultimate,
nostalgic movie out of my system and just

00:05:14.069 --> 00:05:20.949
purge myself, chock full of all this stuff
I’ve always wanted to do.

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:28.770
The things that I’ve loved growing up, all
these obscurities and push everything to the

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:33.129
max from beginning to end and just see where
it lands.

00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:35.860
It was a very polarizing movie when it came
out.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.200
I thought it was a very interesting reaction
that movie got because I was like “Really?

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.280
It was just such a tiny little movie?”

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:45.530
It generated a very polarizing emotion from
people.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:52.030
A lot of people took it very personally they
way they were reacting to it.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:56.080
As long as they react, I don’t care if they
love it or hate it, I just want them to react

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.460
to it and have an opinion about it whether
it’s good or not.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.710
Because if you believe the good reviews, you
have to believe the bad reviews.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:08.210
At a certain point it all just becomes moot.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:13.400
They are all helpful but you should let reviews…the
good reviews don’t matter and the bad reviews

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.400
don’t matter in the grand scheme of things.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.590
But I think it is good to just read them a
little bit.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:24.470
Not be so distant from them because I think
they are taking the time to write about your

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:27.970
movie and they might be crapping all over
it but they are taking the time to write about

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:28.970
it.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:30.449
At the time Hollywood Reporter gave a review
I was like “Wow!

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:32.639
This is a tough, tough business.”

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:42.871
Because they gave a review that was not the
best and they’re not wrong, that’s how

00:06:42.871 --> 00:06:48.500
they feel.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:52.990
Even when they are criticizing it, they are
still complimenting it inside of those criticism

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:58.440
because I think a lot of people thought it
was a much bigger movie.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.480
I don’t think it being a small movie should
ever dictate how harsh you are going to be

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.159
on something, it’s still just the movie
itself.

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:09.879
But it’s just I do think some people thought
it was a really dark movie and it just wasn’t

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:11.470
at all, it was peanuts.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:22.300
I thought it was kind of funny that they were
thinking it was almost like a backhanded compliment.

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:27.520
I think embrace the bad reviews, embrace all
that stuff because it’s just all part of

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.520
making movies.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:31.689
Film Courage: How did you get a distribution
deal?

00:07:31.689 --> 00:07:40.930
Chris: My cinematographer had come across
a sales agent at SXSW in early 2015.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:46.810
He’d asked him if he had any movies he was
working on, horror films and this was one

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:50.940
that we were in soft prep for and then I met
with him right before we were about to shoot

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:57.939
and he switched over to a different company
(a sales agency company) and he kept in touch

00:07:57.939 --> 00:07:58.939
on the movie.

00:07:58.939 --> 00:08:03.130
I was sending him all these stills and he
came to set a couple of times and then we

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:09.860
cut a trailer for the American Film Market
in 2015, which was very much as commercial

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:14.300
of a trailer as we can make it, just very
much like “Straightforward, action, thriller,

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:15.300
cannibal movie.”

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.199
And actually our movie is not that at all.

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:21.729
It has all that stuff in it but it’s a weird
movie.

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:28.520
So we make a trailer to show at AFM and I
get buyers interested and there were a couple

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:32.430
of distributors that he was constantly talking
to for awhile while we were editing the film.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.790
I hated showing work-in-progress versions
of the movie because I was like “No one

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:39.300
is going to truly be able to experience this
movie until it’s mixed.”

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:44.940
It’s just a very specific hybrid, tonal,
poem, kind of like wacked out experience and

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.220
the soundtrack is so paramount for this movie.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:52.990
I just need to finish the movie before you
see what this thing.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.650
And I think that about almost everything,
I never think the movie is good until it’s

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:01.590
finished (I just don’t) and when I say finished
I mean mixed, I think mixing is like the temple

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:05.640
for the movie and I think people underestimate
that a lot, just adjusting volumes but I think

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.990
it’s really much more than that.

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:13.060
All little nuances really dictate the experience.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:19.760
He was really loyal to the film and a couple
months after we finished the movie in April

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:20.760
2016.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.270
We had a little friends and family screening
and couple months later.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.330
We were able to lock in the US distributor.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:33.470
Then learning all about the deliverables,
I remember getting the document and it gives

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.390
you a migraine, your brain starts hurting
reading all these things.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:39.220
You’re just like “This is a nightmare.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:45.730
But then you show it to your sound mixer and
editor and your assistant editor and they

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:47.270
understand it much more clear.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:51.820
It’s just so overly-technical the way it’s
described and it’s so many things I’ve

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:59.630
got 
to send.

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.640
They ended up releasing the movie in February
2017.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:10.440
Australia released the film first, around
Halloween of 2016 (so it was on DVD out there)

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:17.330
and then the following Spring it started to
snowball and go to the US, Canada and Germany

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:18.960
and all these different places.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:25.220
Seeing all the fake posters that Germany made,
some were awesome actually but some were like…I

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:30.210
see them and it’s good artwork but…it’s
so unbelievably…I see what you’re doing

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:32.720
but it’s so not what this movie is at all.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:39.760
I remember one poster said “The Book of
Eli meets whatever…”

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.570
It’s like “Oh, don’t.”

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:48.720
This movie is…it’s a weird acid trip,
David Lynchian kind of thing.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:54.510
It has cool action stuff but it’s not like
this movie that you’re describing to people.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:56.930
I though that was kind of funny.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:01.450
Some people when they see the movie are like
“What the…?”

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:06.620
I think that’s kind of fun though when people
are marketed something and like when DRIVE

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.121
came out everyone was like “FAST AND THE
FURIOUS!”

00:11:09.121 --> 00:11:11.030
And some people were saying “What is this
movie?”

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:13.160
But I think that’s a nice surprise though
sometimes.

